y  very good performance but they may not  generalize well to data that the system  has never seen before the advantage of  course is that you're self organizing  the data using these kinds of approaches  so you can there's a potential for new  scientific insights based on what these  networks are actually learning the  disadvantage though of these deep  networks is that they're computationally  very intensive and they require highly  optimized code and often special  processors to be practical for even  medium sized data sets so when we you  want to train one of these systems on  for example the thousands of hours of  speech data that I referred to earlier  these systems can require thousands of  processors and can run for days at a  time to train so the other I think piece  of the story with deep learning is we  finally have vast computational power  that's inexpensive enough that lots of  researchers have access to it and that's  really allowed the field to take off the  the use of special processors such as  GPUs has made these systems  computationally feasible and that's one  of the reasons why you see so much work  in this area now finally the compute  resources are available so that these  systems can be applied without having to  have a supercomputer but through this  process we I want to come back to this  issue of annotation of data unsupervised  training is really critical in this  field because you don't really have data  which is annotated the way you would  really like if the world were perfect  we'd have lots of EEG data annotated at  the event level localized in time but we  never really have that all that we  actually have is a report and I'm  showing an example of a report here  these reports are essentially  unstructured text even in today's EMR  electronic medical record systems a lot  of the data is still unstructured and  the language that's used is indirect and  purposefully defensive so they will say  things like show  indications of rather t