IH bd2k grant titled automatic  discovery and processing of EEG cohorts  from clinical records so that grant of  the collaboration between Temple  University and UT Dallas so with that  I'd like to turn it over to dr. pukani  and and first of all and thank you very  much for coming to give us this lecture  today I'm really looking forward to it  great ok well good good morning or  afternoon we're really excited to have a  chance to talk data wrangling to people  today and really appreciate him the  hosting the seminar so we can get a  chance to have this meeting what I would  like to do today is to talk mostly about  how important data wrangling is to this  whole area of machine learning and big  data data wrangling is defined as the  process of mapping data from an  unstructured format to another format  that enables automated processing so  that's a pretty vague statement I think  but today what we're going to do is  we're going to focus on data wrangling  of physiological signals because these  posed some rather unique challenges  compared to text data or the other types  of data that you find and in a lot of  machine learning applications signal  data is always a bit more challenging  because of the nature of the signals so  we'll focus today on signal data and  particularly we'll spend some time  talking about EE G's and then the next  talk will focus more on the text side  how we deal with medical reports and how  we deal with unstructured text data  which also plays a role in this because  we need both sources of data to actually  do automated data wrangling of these  types of signals so let's begin then by  talking a little bit about Big Data and  machine learning for many years now  we've had a fairly well-defined  iterative process to developing this  kind of technology which we might call a  classic machine learning learning  paradigm  it begins with collection of data data I  hope if there's one message I'll leave  you with today  it's how important data is to solving  these kinds of problems because if the  data is not representative of the  problem then most of the research done  is really not that valuable and making  the data representative of the problem  is also much more challenging than  people think so we'll talk quite a bit  today about data and the associated  issues with data such as annotation but  we begin with data we typically select  some features and we're always worried  of course about whether these features  capture meaningful differences between  the patterns we then select some model  or we might try many models but we  always worry how do we find the best  model we'll then go through a training  process where we estimate the parameters  of the model and try to optimize the  model on the data and then we'll  evaluate the classifier and if all goes  well that evaluation will give us  insight into the data itself and very  often we end up then recollecting or re  annotating the data and beginning the  process all over again we'll also talk  today a little bit about how important  evaluation metrics are to these problems  and how important it is to make sure  that as you optimize the technology  you're optimizing it on things that are  meaningful to the end users of the  technology but this is sort of a closed  loop process because we can iterate on  this many times and the technology today  is sufficiently powerful enough that if  we're if we train on the same data that  we evaluate on we can usually drive  error rates very close to zero which is  very satisfying but that doesn't mean  that the system necessarily generalizes  well to new data sets so we'll talk a  little bit about  that problem as well other things to  think about often when you do these  kinds of optimizations you end up with  technology which is very application  specific and data dependent what's even  worse though is that customers or users  don't often completely understand the  requirements so very often after you  develop the first version of the system  you find out that that's not really what  the customers wanted and then you  iterate through several times in fact on  EEG data that we'll talk about we're  actually on our fourth generation of  trying to make the system do something  that's relevant to the users and it's  been a learning process for the users as  well as for the technology developers  and data collection is often an ongoing  process that drives a technology in  usually we like to collect data that  allows us to solve specific problems but  you can really can't do that all at once  so every year we try to collect data  that pushes the technology a little  further and we've had an evolution like  that in fields like speech and image  processing we're just beginning that  process in areas like bioengineering  particularly areas like EEG s and such  so why is big data so important I think  many of you have probably seen a lot of  press in recent years about the progress  that's been made on some very visible  tasks such as speech recognition in the  in the past five years alone we've seen  significant improvement in performance  of these systems based on what are  called deep learning systems it's been a  very very exciting very active time  because of this to put this in  perspective on tasks which we were  scoring 80% error rate that's 80% error  rate in the 1990s this is a task based  on conversational speech recognition  today we're forint we're scoring error  rates in the range of about seven  percent the performance on these tasks  is getting very close to human  performance and that didn't happen  overnight however that took about 50  years of research to get to that point  but progress been significant in the  last five years and that's primarily  been due to the emergence learning  systems coupled with the availability of  large amounts of data so this is a slide  I want people to really pay attention to  that deep learning systems require large  amounts of data we can get very good  performance with these systems but we  need lots of data and this is one of a  number of examples that have been cited  in recent years here we have a task  where we compare what was conventional  technology maybe 10 years ago most of  most of commercial systems were based on  a technology called hidden Markov models  and here we have a deep learning system  and on a fairly moderate amount of data  you very often see that these deep  learning systems perform comparable e to  the existing technology but you don't  see big wins with these systems because  these deep learning systems have many  many parameters associated with them and  they take a long time to train on the  data and you just need lots of data  however when we do have lots of data so  when we go from for example about 10  hours of speech data to about 2000 hours  of speech data we begin to see very  significant differences in performance  between the two so that's a good that's  good news in some sense because it says  if we have adequate resources to develop  the technology we can get very very good  performance but this is a very sobering  issue for biomedical or bioengineering  applications because it's very rare that  such applications have the equivalent of  2,000 hours of speech data and I'll talk  a little bit about that in a moment  big data is everywhere  a couple favorite quotes of ours in the  speech research community which I've  worked in for many years we actually  back in the mid 80s a researcher at IBM  Bob Mercer coined a phrase which has  been very popular which is there's no  data like more data with new data you  always learn new things and you always  worry about the Syst the ability of your  system to generalize so it's always good  it never hurts to have more data  more recently you've heard a lot about  big data and here's one of the more  famous quotes about big data there's  everybody talks about it but in reality  when you start looking at the datasets  available in the community for various  bioengineering tests you very often find  that these datasets are small they may  consist of tens of subjects or even a  hundred of subjects but they don't  consist of thousands or tens of  thousands or millions that we need to  train these big data systems and and  that's something that we're attempting  to change there is why are we so  interested in this well there is there's  a belief that if we can actually build  systems that look over large amounts of  data we can develop new science we can  uncover new relationships and  dependencies or we can improve the  ability of systems to diagnose and oh I  want to come back to that issue because  that's an interesting issue when we talk  about areas like EEG s or pathology that  require significant amounts of expertise  so where do we get this kind of data  well in commercially viable applications  there are significant economic  motivation so in an application like  speech recognition where billions of  dollars are being invested in  development of the technology there's a  good justification for creating large  data resources and to some extent since  everybody understands language we all  use it every day the data is quite  abundant in bioengineering applications  however the commercial opportunities are  not so significant it's not to say that  they're not important they're of course  very important but when you start  dealing with financial issues and and  such there's less willingness to invest  the amount large amounts of money in  collection of data so the development of  data resources is lagging however there  is enormous amounts of data available in  the healthcare industry so I'll talk  today about a database we've been  building over the past four years which  is a known as a tuh EEG corpus and it  essentially consists of the last  fourteen years of each  has collected a temple hospital we're  now about 30,000 sessions over 16,000  patients and it's a very rich v